---
// Long-tail SEO page: "run python as a service".
// Drop into: src/pages/serv/run-python-as-a-service.astro  →  /serv/run-python-as-a-service/
import ServLayout from '../../layouts/ServLayout.astro';

const canonical = 'https://tillmanbuildstech.com/serv/run-python-as-a-service/';
const title = 'How to run a Python script as a service (Windows, Linux, macOS) — Serv';
const description = 'Turn any Python script into a supervised, auto-restarting native service with Serv. One YAML config installs a Windows service, a systemd unit, or a launchd job.';
---

<ServLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <section class="serv-hero">
    <p class="serv-label">// guide</p>
    <h1>Run a Python script as a service</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      You've got a Python app — an API server, a worker, a scraper — and you need it
      running all the time, surviving reboots and crashes, with logs you can actually read. That's what
      a service is for. Serv turns <code>python app.py</code> into a native, supervised service on
      Windows, Linux, or macOS.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="serv-label">// why not a terminal</p>
    <h2>Why not just run it in a terminal?</h2>
    <p>
      A terminal process dies when you close the session, doesn't come back after a reboot, and leaves
      no restart policy. A real service is started by the OS, restarts on failure, and runs whether or not
      anyone is logged in.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="serv-label">// install &amp; start</p>
    <h2>Get serv, then install and start it</h2>
    <p>
      First, install the <code>serv</code> CLI once —
      <a href="/serv/#install">one line for your OS</a>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>winget install TillmanBuildsTech.Serv   # Windows (or: choco install serv)
brew install serv                       # macOS
npm install -g @tillmanbuildstech/serv  # any OS with Node.js</code></pre>
    <p>Then register your script as a service:</p>
    <pre><code>serv install --exe /usr/bin/python3 --name mypy --args "-u /srv/app/app.py"
serv start mypy
serv status mypy</code></pre>
    <p>That's it. Serv registers the script as a native service, starts it, and supervises it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="serv-label">// supervision for free</p>
    <h2>Supervision you get for free</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Auto-restart with backoff</strong> — if the script crashes, Serv restarts it with exponential backoff so it doesn't hot-loop.</li>
      <li><strong>Graceful shutdown</strong> — sends SIGTERM (Windows: Ctrl+C escalation) so your script can clean up.</li>
      <li><strong>Log capture &amp; rotation</strong> — stdout/stderr are captured and rotated, no more lost tracebacks.</li>
      <li><strong>Lifecycle hooks</strong> — run setup before start or cleanup after exit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Prefer a YAML config? Serv reads the same settings from a file — see the
      <a href="https://github.com/TillmanBuildsTech/serv/blob/main/docs/configuration.md">configuration reference</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="serv-label">// alternatives</p>
    <h2>Alternatives</h2>
    <p>
      On Windows you might reach for <a href="/serv/nssm-alternative/">NSSM</a>. Serv is a modern,
      cross-platform replacement that uses the same YAML everywhere.
    </p>
  </section>
</ServLayout>
